/* Original teaching material; outcome references point to NCCA Junior Cycle Business Studies. */
const STRANDS = [
 {id:'finance',name:'Personal Finance',icon:'€',title:'Make your money make sense',description:'Everyday choices. A little more confidence.',color:'#087f68'},
 {id:'enterprise',name:'Enterprise',icon:'↗',title:'Turn a good idea into a business',description:'Think creatively. Plan carefully. Make it happen.',color:'#7b67af'},
 {id:'economy',name:'Our Economy',icon:'◎',title:'See the bigger picture',description:'Connect your choices to Ireland and the world.',color:'#c38834'}
];
const LESSONS = [];
function add(id,strand,title,icon,outcomes,summary,notes,example,questions){LESSONS.push({id,strand,title,icon,outcomes,summary,notes,example,questions:questions.map((q,i)=>({id:id+'-'+i,prompt:q[0],options:q[1],answer:q[2],explanation:q[3]}))});}
add('needs','finance','Needs, wants & smart choices','★','1.1, 1.3','Make the most of the resources you have.',
'A need is essential for living; a want makes life more enjoyable. Your resources include money, time and skills. Choosing one use for a limited resource means giving up another. Financial needs also change as you move from school to work, a family or retirement.',
'Aoife has €30. A bus pass to school costs €20 and a game costs €25. Paying for essential travel first leaves €10 to save towards the game.',[
['Which is the clearest example of a need?',['Safe drinking water','A new games console','Designer runners','A music subscription'],0,'Water is essential for living. The other items may be enjoyable, but they are wants.'],
['Aoife has €30 and buys a €20 bus pass. How much remains?',['€50','€20','€10','€5'],2,'€30 − €20 = €10. Check what remains before making another purchase.'],
['Which is a personal resource other than money?',['A shop’s profit','Your time and skills','National debt','The rate of inflation'],1,'Time and skills can help you achieve goals even when money is limited.'],
['Which need is most closely linked to retirement planning?',['Buying a school uniform','Booking a school trip','Funding a first driving lesson','Building income for later life'],3,'A financial lifecycle helps you plan for needs at different stages of your life.']]);
add('budget','finance','Build a brilliant budget','▤','1.2, 1.12, 1.13','Give every euro a job.',
'A budget is a plan of expected income and expenditure for a period. Income is money coming in; expenditure is money going out. A surplus occurs when income exceeds expenditure. A deficit means expenditure is greater. Compare actual figures with your plan and adjust flexible spending first.',
'Weekly income: €60. Travel €15 + lunch €20 + entertainment €10 = €45 expenditure. Surplus = €60 − €45 = €15.',[
['What is a budget?',['A record of profit only','A plan of income and expenditure','A type of loan','A list of possessions'],1,'A budget looks ahead at the money you expect to receive and spend.'],
['Income is €80 and expenditure is €95. What is the result?',['€15 surplus','€175 surplus','€15 deficit','€95 deficit'],2,'€80 − €95 = −€15, so expenditure exceeds income by €15.'],
['Which is usually the most flexible household expense?',['Rent due under a lease','An agreed loan repayment','A fixed insurance payment','Optional cinema trips'],3,'Optional entertainment is usually easier to reduce than a committed payment.'],
['You budget €20 for lunches but spend €27. By how much are you over budget?',['€7','€47','€20','€27'],0,'Actual spending €27 − planned spending €20 = €7 over budget.']]);
add('saving','finance','Save today, borrow wisely','◇','1.5, 1.8','Compare the cost, not just the weekly payment.',
'Saving puts money aside for a goal or an unexpected cost. Borrowing gives you money now that you repay, usually with interest. Compare total repayments, fees, term and APR when choosing a loan. A longer term can reduce each instalment while increasing total cost. Simple interest = principal × rate × time.',
'€200 saved for one year at an illustrative simple interest rate of 3% earns €200 × 0.03 × 1 = €6. The balance is €206. Rates here are examples, not live offers.',[
['Simple interest on €200 at 3% for one year is…',['€60','€3','€203','€6'],3,'€200 × 3 ÷ 100 = €6 interest.'],
['A €500 loan requires €560 total repayments, with no other fees. Cost of credit?',['€560','€60','€500','€1,060'],1,'Cost of credit = total repayments − amount borrowed = €60.'],
['Why build an emergency savings fund?',['To prepare for unexpected costs','To guarantee a profit on shares','To avoid all future expenses','To make borrowing free'],0,'A buffer can help meet an unexpected bill without immediately borrowing.'],
['Which is the strongest loan comparison?',['The colour of the bank logo','Only the first weekly payment','APR, fees, total cost and affordability','Only the advertising slogan'],2,'Compare both the overall cost and whether you can manage repayments.']]);
add('payslip','finance','Decode your payslip','€','1.4, 1.11','From gross pay to money in your account.',
'Gross pay is pay before deductions. Net pay is gross pay less total deductions. Irish payslips may show PAYE Income Tax, USC and PRSI. A tax credit reduces calculated Income Tax. For classroom calculations, use the rates and credits supplied in the question; these examples are not current tax bands.',
'Illustrative payslip: gross €500; PAYE €40, USC €10, PRSI €20. Deductions total €70, so net pay is €430.',[
['Gross pay is…',['Pay before deductions','The amount left after tax','Only your overtime','The employer’s profit'],0,'Gross pay is the total pay before deductions are taken.'],
['Gross pay €500, deductions €70. Net pay?',['€570','€500','€430','€70'],2,'Net pay = €500 − €70 = €430.'],
['In a simplified exercise, tax before credits is €90 and tax credits are €30. Income Tax due?',['€120','€60','€30','€90'],1,'Tax credits reduce the calculated Income Tax: €90 − €30 = €60.'],
['What should you do with the rates in a classroom tax question?',['Replace them with a guess','Use the rates from a different year','Ignore the credits','Use the supplied rates and credits'],3,'Use the information supplied. Real tax calculations depend on current rules and personal circumstances.']]);
add('consumer','finance','Be a confident consumer','▣','1.7, 1.8','Know what to do when a purchase goes wrong.',
'Consumers should compare offers, use products properly and keep proof of purchase. If goods are faulty, contact the seller and explain the issue. The remedy depends on circumstances such as timing and seriousness. The CCPC provides consumer information in Ireland. A shop’s change-of-mind policy is different from rights for faulty goods.',
'Your new headphones stop working during normal use. Keep your proof of purchase, explain the fault to the retailer and request an appropriate remedy. Check the CCPC’s current guidance.',[
['Who should you normally contact first about faulty goods?',['A different retailer','The business that sold them','The school office','A social media follower'],1,'The seller is responsible for resolving faults covered by consumer law.'],
['Which is good evidence of a purchase?',['The colour of your bag','A friend’s guess','The shop’s opening hours','A receipt or payment record'],3,'Keep a receipt or other proof of purchase to support your complaint.'],
['Which body offers consumer information in Ireland?',['CCPC','ECB','A local sports club','The weather service'],0,'The Competition and Consumer Protection Commission provides consumer information.'],
['Which statement is most accurate?',['Every return has identical rules','Misuse always creates a refund right','Faulty-goods rights differ from change-of-mind policies','Sale goods never have consumer rights'],2,'A faulty product and a change of mind are different situations. Always check the relevant guidance.']]);
add('insurance','finance','Protect what matters','⬡','1.6','Understand risk before it becomes a bill.',
'Insurance transfers a specified financial risk to an insurer in exchange for a premium. A policy describes the cover, exclusions and conditions. An excess is the amount you contribute to a covered claim. Compare cover as well as price, and give accurate information when taking out insurance.',
'An illustrative covered repair costs €600. If the policy excess is €100 and no other limit applies, the policyholder pays €100 and the insurer pays €500.',[
['What is an insurance premium?',['The price paid for insurance','The value of every claim','The insurer’s logo','A guaranteed refund'],0,'The premium is the payment for the cover agreed in the policy.'],
['Covered damage €600, excess €100. How much does the insurer pay in this example?',['€700','€600','€100','€500'],3,'€600 − €100 excess = €500, assuming the rest is covered.'],
['Which should you compare before choosing a policy?',['Only the advert','Cover, exclusions, excess and premium','Only the name','Only the first letter of the policy'],1,'Cheap cover may exclude something you need. Read the conditions.'],
['An exclusion is…',['The full amount of cover','A payment for renewing','A risk or situation the policy does not cover','A savings bonus'],2,'Exclusions describe circumstances outside the policy’s cover.']]);
add('sustainable','finance','Shop with the future in mind','♧','1.9, 1.10','Think beyond the price tag.',
'Sustainable consumption considers social and environmental effects as well as price. Repairing, reusing and buying durable goods can reduce waste. Ethical choices may consider working conditions and fair pay. Global trade and online shopping expand choice but may create delivery, privacy and environmental concerns.',
'A €40 school bag lasts four years: €10 per year. A €20 bag replaced every year costs €80 over four years. Durability can offer better value and create less waste.',[
['A €40 bag lasts four years. Cost per year?',['€40','€160','€10','€4'],2,'€40 ÷ 4 years = €10 per year.'],
['Which action supports a circular economy?',['Repairing a usable bicycle','Discarding working equipment','Replacing goods every week','Avoiding all reuse'],0,'Repair keeps products and materials useful for longer.'],
['An ethical purchasing consideration is…',['Only the packaging colour','Only the delivery speed','Only the brand font','Working conditions of producers'],3,'Ethical consumption considers how people are treated during production.'],
['A possible downside of global online shopping is…',['No product choice','Transport emissions and difficult returns','No need for delivery','Guaranteed local jobs'],1,'More choice can come with environmental and practical trade-offs.']]);
add('finance-case','finance','Money mission: the school trip','⚑','1.1, 1.5, 1.12, 1.13','Bring your personal finance skills together.',
'Apply a simple routine: identify your goal, list income and costs, calculate the balance, then recommend a realistic action. Explain why your recommendation works. For savings targets, divide the remaining amount by the number of weeks available.',
'Cian needs €180 for a trip and has €60 saved. He needs another €120. With eight weeks to go, he must save €15 per week.',[
['Cian needs €180 and has €60 saved. How much more is needed?',['€240','€60','€180','€120'],3,'€180 − €60 = €120 still needed.'],
['How much should Cian save weekly to reach €120 in eight weeks?',['€8','€15','€20','€12'],1,'€120 ÷ 8 = €15 each week.'],
['Cian receives €30 weekly and spends €22. What is his current weekly surplus?',['€8','€52','€22','€30'],0,'Income €30 − expenditure €22 = €8 surplus.'],
['How can he reach the €15 weekly target from an €8 surplus?',['Spend an extra €7','Borrow without checking costs','Reduce optional spending by €7 weekly','Stop recording spending'],2,'€8 + €7 saved from spending = the €15 weekly target.']]);
add('entrepreneur','enterprise','Start with a spark','ϟ','2.1, 2.2','Spot a problem. Imagine a solution.',
'An entrepreneur identifies an opportunity and organises resources to act on it, taking a calculated risk. Useful qualities include initiative, resilience and creativity. Financial enterprises aim to earn profit. Social and cultural enterprises pursue social or cultural goals and may also earn income.',
'A school team notices unused uniforms and starts a uniform exchange. It solves a problem, reduces waste and helps families. Its social purpose matters alongside covering its costs.',[
['An entrepreneur is someone who…',['Avoids all decisions','Identifies opportunities and organises resources','Only buys shares','Never faces risk'],1,'Entrepreneurs turn opportunities into action by bringing resources together.'],
['Learning from a failed first attempt shows…',['Resilience','Inflation','Expenditure','An import'],0,'Resilience means adapting and continuing after setbacks.'],
['A social enterprise mainly aims to…',['Avoid all income','Hide its purpose','Make every service free','Address a social or community need'],3,'Social enterprises use business activity to pursue a social purpose.'],
['A calculated risk means…',['Ignoring possible losses','Expecting guaranteed success','Weighing potential rewards against possible losses','Spending without a plan'],2,'Use evidence and planning to make an informed decision despite uncertainty.']]);
add('research','enterprise','Ask before you launch','⌕','2.7','Find out what your customers actually need.',
'Primary research collects new information, such as a survey or interview. Secondary research uses existing information, such as published statistics. A useful sample reflects your target customers. Avoid leading questions and distinguish what the evidence shows from your assumptions.',
'A student café surveys 40 pupils: 26 prefer fruit smoothies. That is 26 ÷ 40 × 100 = 65%. Surveying only the café team’s friends could bias the result.',[
['Which is primary market research?',['Reading an old report','Using a published chart','Running a customer survey','Reading a competitor’s website'],2,'A new survey collects original information for your research.'],
['26 of 40 pupils prefer smoothies. What percentage is that?',['65%','26%','40%','15%'],0,'Divide the number who prefer smoothies by the full sample: 26 ÷ 40 × 100 = 65%.'],
['Which survey question is least leading?',['You love our amazing idea, right?','How much do you love our perfect product?','Surely you would buy this?','Which of these drinks would you choose?'],3,'A neutral question avoids encouraging a particular answer.'],
['Why survey people from different year groups?',['To guarantee everyone agrees','To get a more representative sample','To avoid collecting data','To remove all uncertainty'],1,'A broader relevant sample can better reflect the target market.']]);
add('marketing','enterprise','Make your product stand out','✦','2.8','Meet the four Ps of marketing.',
'The marketing mix includes Product, Price, Place and Promotion. Product covers features and customer benefits. Price should consider costs, competitors and what customers will pay. Place is how the product reaches customers. Promotion communicates the offer. Keep the mix suitable for your target market.',
'A reusable school bottle: leakproof design (Product), €8 (Price), sold at school (Place), promoted with a poster and demonstration (Promotion).',[
['Which list contains the four Ps?',['Profit, People, Pay, Policy','Product, Price, Place, Promotion','Product, Profit, Price, Pension','Place, Payroll, People, Profit'],1,'Product, Price, Place and Promotion work together as the marketing mix.'],
['Selling through a school pop-up shop is a decision about…',['Price','Promotion','Product','Place'],3,'Place means distribution: where and how the customer can buy.'],
['A leakproof bottle lid is part of…',['Product','Price','Place','Promotion'],0,'A feature or benefit belongs to the Product part of the mix.'],
['The target market is…',['Every possible person equally','Only the business owner','The group of customers the offer is designed for','The amount of tax paid'],2,'Understanding a specific customer group helps shape a suitable marketing mix.']]);
add('plan','enterprise','Give your idea a plan','▤','2.9, 2.11','A good idea deserves a realistic route forward.',
'A business plan sets out the idea, objectives, target market, marketing, operations and financial forecasts. Objectives should be specific and measurable. Choose finance suited to the purpose and repayment ability. Short-term needs and long-term equipment purchases may need different funding.',
'A school enterprise plans to sell 80 notebooks at €3 in one month. Its plan identifies customers, suppliers, promotion, costs and what it will do if sales are lower than expected.',[
['Which objective is easiest to measure?',['Be the best ever','Do better somehow','Sell 80 notebooks within one month','Make everyone happy'],2,'This objective gives a number and a time period for checking progress.'],
['Why include forecasts in a business plan?',['To estimate income, costs and funding needs','To guarantee success','To remove every risk','To replace market research'],0,'Forecasts help assess whether the idea is financially workable.'],
['Which finance is generally suited to a brief cash shortfall, subject to costs and approval?',['A 25-year loan for every bill','Selling every asset immediately','Ignoring suppliers','A short-term overdraft facility'],3,'An overdraft can cover a temporary shortfall, but interest, limits and repayment still matter.'],
['A contingency plan describes…',['Only the logo','What to do if events differ from expectations','Only last year’s sales','A guaranteed profit'],1,'Planning alternatives makes it easier to respond when assumptions change.']]);
add('people','enterprise','People make a business','♙','2.3, 2.4, 2.5, 2.6','Work well, act fairly, think responsibly.',
'Employment is paid work under an employment relationship; volunteering is work freely done without wages. Employers and employees both have responsibilities, including workplace safety and agreed duties. Business decisions affect customers, workers, communities and the environment. Technology can improve productivity but brings costs and training needs.',
'A local bakery starts online orders. Customers gain convenience, but staff need training and the bakery must manage setup costs and customer data responsibly.',[
['Which best describes volunteering?',['Any paid overtime','Work freely done without wages','A business loan','Only work done at home'],1,'Volunteering contributes time and effort without wages.'],
['Who should contribute to a safe workplace?',['Employers and employees','Only customers','Only the newest worker','Nobody if the business is small'],0,'Employers have safety duties, and employees must also cooperate and work safely.'],
['A stakeholder in a local bakery could be…',['Only its cash register','Only its oven','Only its logo','A neighbour affected by deliveries'],3,'Stakeholders are people or groups affected by or able to affect the organisation.'],
['A realistic effect of new business technology is…',['No training is ever needed','It always removes all costs','Efficiency benefits alongside costs and training needs','It guarantees higher profits'],2,'Evaluate both benefits and costs before recommending a technology change.']]);
add('documents','enterprise','Follow the paper trail','▱','2.10','From an order to a payment.',
'A quotation offers prices and terms. A purchase order requests goods. A delivery note lists goods delivered. An invoice requests payment and shows amounts due. A credit note reduces a customer’s balance, for example for returned goods. A receipt confirms payment. Check quantities, prices and calculations.',
'A café orders 12 mugs at €4 each. The goods total is €48 before any stated tax or delivery charge. If two mugs are returned and credited at €4 each, the balance falls by €8.',[
['Which document requests payment?',['A delivery note','A receipt','An invoice','A quotation request'],2,'An invoice tells the customer what is owed for the transaction.'],
['Which document confirms payment was received?',['A receipt','A purchase order','A quotation','An unpaid invoice'],0,'A receipt is evidence that a payment has been made.'],
['Two mugs at €4 each are returned. What credit is due, ignoring tax and delivery?',['€2','€4','€12','€8'],3,'2 × €4 = €8, so the balance reduces by €8.'],
['What should you compare with a delivery note?',['The weather forecast','The goods received and the original order','Only the shop’s logo','Only your personal budget'],1,'Check that the quantity and type of goods received match the order.']]);
add('cash','enterprise','Keep the cash flowing','⇄','2.11, 2.12','Profit is useful. Cash pays the bills.',
'A cash-flow forecast estimates money received and paid over time. Closing cash = opening cash + receipts − payments. Profit and cash differ: a credit sale can earn profit before the customer pays. In double entry, a cash receipt increases the cash account with a debit; its other side is a credit to the relevant account. A trial balance lists ledger balances to check debit and credit totals.',
'Opening cash €100 + receipts €450 − payments €380 = closing cash €170. A cash sale of €50 is recorded as debit Cash €50 and credit Sales €50.',[
['Opening cash €100, receipts €450, payments €380. Closing cash?',['€930','€170','€70','€830'],1,'€100 + €450 − €380 = €170.'],
['Why can a profitable business run short of cash?',['Profit always equals bank balance','Cash never matters','Every sale is cash','Customers may pay after bills fall due'],3,'Credit sales can create a time gap between earning revenue and receiving cash.'],
['The cash-account entry for receiving €50 cash from a sale is…',['Debit Cash €50','Credit Cash €50','Ignore Cash','Debit Cash €100'],0,'Cash is an asset. An increase is a debit; the matching entry is a credit to Sales.'],
['Equal trial balance totals prove…',['Every transaction is correct','No entry is missing','Total debit balances equal total credit balances','The business has cash to pay every bill'],2,'Agreement checks arithmetic balance, but omissions and some other errors can still exist.']]);
add('accounts','enterprise','Did the business make a profit?','◴','2.13','Read the story behind the numbers.',
'Cost of sales = opening inventory + purchases − closing inventory. Gross profit = sales − cost of sales. Net profit = gross profit − operating expenses. A statement of financial position shows assets, liabilities and equity at a date. Assets = liabilities + equity. Figures help you recommend action, not just calculate totals.',
'Sales €1,000; cost of sales €600; expenses €250. Gross profit is €400 and net profit €150. Assets of €900 and liabilities of €300 leave equity of €600.',[
['Sales €1,000 less cost of sales €600 gives gross profit of…',['€1,600','€600','€400','€150'],2,'Gross profit = €1,000 − €600 = €400.'],
['Gross profit €400, expenses €250. Net profit?',['€150','€650','€250','€400'],0,'Net profit = €400 − €250 = €150.'],
['Opening inventory €100, purchases €500, closing inventory €150. Cost of sales?',['€750','€550','€350','€450'],3,'€100 + €500 − €150 = €450. Unsold closing inventory is not yet cost of sales.'],
['Assets €900, liabilities €300. Equity?',['€1,200','€600','€300','€900'],1,'Equity = assets − liabilities = €900 − €300 = €600.']]);
add('scarcity','economy','Choices shape our economy','◎','3.1, 3.2','Limited resources. Unlimited possibilities.',
'Scarcity means resources are limited relative to wants. Opportunity cost is the next best alternative forgone when you choose. Factors of production are land, labour, capital and enterprise. Households provide resources and buy goods; businesses produce; government provides services and influences how resources are used.',
'A council has a site that could become a library or a sports centre. If it chooses the library, the opportunity cost is the next best use it gives up: the sports centre.',[
['Scarcity exists because…',['Resources are unlimited','Resources are limited relative to wants','Everyone has identical wants','Money can buy everything'],1,'Limited time, money and natural resources force people to choose.'],
['Opportunity cost is…',['Every alternative added together','Always the cash price','A tax on choices','The next best alternative forgone'],3,'It is the best alternative you give up when making a choice.'],
['Which is an example of capital as a factor of production?',['A machine used to make goods','A customer’s birthday','Rainfall alone','Only a company name'],0,'Capital includes human-made resources used in production, such as machinery.'],
['Households contribute to the economy by…',['Only receiving government spending','Never supplying resources','Supplying labour and buying goods and services','Setting every business price'],2,'Households participate as consumers and as providers of productive resources.']]);
add('markets','economy','Why do prices change?','↗','3.3','Get to know demand and supply.',
'Demand is the quantity consumers are willing and able to buy at different prices. Supply is the quantity producers are willing and able to sell. Other things equal, higher demand tends to raise the market price; higher supply tends to lower it. Equilibrium is where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.',
'A popular concert has a fixed number of seats. If more people want tickets while supply stays the same, there is upward pressure on the price.',[
['If demand increases and supply is unchanged, price generally…',['Tends to fall','Must remain fixed','Tends to rise','Becomes zero'],2,'More buyers competing for the same supply puts upward pressure on price.'],
['If a good harvest increases supply while demand is unchanged, price generally…',['Tends to fall','Must double','Always rises','Stops existing'],0,'More output available to buyers puts downward pressure on price.'],
['Market equilibrium occurs where…',['All wants are satisfied','Supply is always zero','Government owns every firm','Quantity demanded equals quantity supplied'],3,'At equilibrium the quantities buyers and sellers plan match.'],
['In economics, demand requires…',['Wanting something only','Willingness and ability to buy','Only seeing an advert','Owning a factory'],1,'A want becomes market demand when the consumer is also able to buy.']]);
add('government','economy','Where public money goes','▥','3.4, 3.5','Follow the money behind public services.',
'Government revenue includes taxes and other receipts. Expenditure pays for services, infrastructure and supports. Current expenditure covers ongoing costs; capital expenditure creates or improves long-term assets. Taxes help fund services and can influence behaviour and redistribute income. A budget deficit occurs when expenditure exceeds revenue; debt is the outstanding stock of borrowing.',
'In a simplified budget, revenue of €90 billion and expenditure of €95 billion create a €5 billion deficit. Building a school is capital spending; paying teachers is current spending.',[
['Which is government revenue?',['Income Tax collected','A hospital construction payment','A teacher’s public salary','A road repair bill'],0,'Tax received is money coming into government finances.'],
['Building a new public school is mainly…',['Personal saving','An import only','A household budget','Capital expenditure'],3,'A school building is a long-term asset.'],
['Revenue €90bn, spending €95bn. Budget result?',['€5bn surplus','€5bn deficit','€185bn surplus','€95bn deficit'],1,'Spending exceeds revenue by €5bn.'],
['Which best distinguishes deficit and debt?',['They always mean exactly the same thing','Debt is only this year’s spending','A deficit is a period’s shortfall; debt is outstanding borrowing','A deficit means no borrowing is outstanding'],2,'Deficit is a flow over a period; debt is a stock at a point in time.']]);
add('inflation','economy','Prices, jobs & interest rates','◷','3.9','Understand the economic news.',
'Inflation is a sustained increase in the general price level. It reduces what a fixed amount of money can buy. Interest is the cost of borrowing or a return on saving. Higher interest rates can make borrowing more expensive. Employment and unemployment indicators describe the labour market; unemployment rates refer to the labour force, not the whole population.',
'A basket rises from €100 to €105. Its price increase is (€5 ÷ €100) × 100 = 5%. This single basket is a classroom illustration, not a current national inflation figure.',[
['A basket rises from €100 to €105. Percentage increase?',['105%','€5%','5%','50%'],2,'Increase €5 ÷ original €100 × 100 = 5%.'],
['If prices rise while your income stays fixed, purchasing power…',['Falls','Always doubles','Must rise','Is unaffected'],0,'The same amount of money buys fewer goods and services.'],
['Higher loan interest rates generally…',['Make every loan free','Remove repayment obligations','Guarantee higher wages','Increase borrowing costs'],3,'Higher interest means a greater cost for borrowing, depending on the loan’s terms.'],
['The unemployment rate is usually measured relative to…',['All children','The labour force','All retirees','All tourists'],1,'The labour force includes those employed and those unemployed who are seeking and available for work.']]);
add('growth','economy','Growth that lasts','♧','3.6','A stronger economy needs a longer view.',
'Economic growth means an increase in real output over time. It can raise incomes and create jobs, but may also increase pollution or resource use. Sustainable development considers present needs and future generations. Evaluate economic, social and environmental effects together rather than assuming all growth is equally beneficial.',
'A factory expansion creates 50 jobs but increases waste. A useful recommendation considers jobs alongside measures such as cleaner equipment, waste reduction and monitoring.',[
['Economic growth is best described as…',['Any price increase','An increase in real output','Only a rise in imports','Always a fall in employment'],1,'Real output measures production after allowing for price changes.'],
['A potential benefit of growth is…',['Guaranteed equal incomes','No use of resources','No environmental effects','More employment opportunities'],3,'Expansion can create jobs, although benefits are not always shared equally.'],
['Sustainable development considers…',['Present needs and future generations','Only this week’s profit','Only the number of adverts','Only the cheapest materials'],0,'Long-term social and environmental needs matter alongside economic benefits.'],
['Which response best evaluates a new factory?',['Count jobs and ignore everything else','Reject every factory automatically','Weigh jobs, community effects and environmental costs','Assume no trade-offs exist'],2,'A balanced judgement uses more than a single indicator.']]);
add('trade','economy','Ireland trades with the world','⇄','3.7','Imports, exports and a connected economy.',
'An export is a good or service sold abroad. An import is bought from abroad. International trade can widen choice, create markets and support specialisation. It can also expose businesses to overseas competition, transport costs and supply disruptions. Globalisation increases links between economies.',
'An Irish design studio selling a service to a French customer earns an export. An Irish shop buying Italian shoes makes an import. Exports €120m − imports €100m = €20m trade surplus in a simplified goods example.',[
['An Irish firm sells software to a German customer. This is…',['An import to Ireland','A household expense only','An Irish export','A government grant'],2,'Selling a service abroad is an export from Ireland.'],
['An Irish retailer buys Spanish oranges. This is…',['An Irish import','An Irish export','Domestic production','A tax credit'],0,'The oranges are bought from abroad, so they are imports.'],
['Exports €120m, imports €100m. Simplified trade balance?',['€220m deficit','€20m deficit','€100m surplus','€20m surplus'],3,'€120m − €100m = €20m surplus.'],
['A possible challenge of global trade is…',['No international competition','Supply disruption in another country','No transport costs','Guaranteed sales'],1,'International supply chains can be affected by events far from Ireland.']]);
add('eu','economy','Ireland & the European Union','◉','3.8','Part of a bigger neighbourhood.',
'The EU single market supports movement of goods, services, people and capital, subject to its rules. Ireland uses the euro. The European Central Bank manages monetary policy for the euro area. EU membership can provide market access and opportunities to work or study abroad, alongside obligations, competition and shared decisions.',
'An Irish small business can reach customers across the EU. It still needs to understand applicable product, consumer and tax rules. A shared currency reduces currency exchange within the euro area.',[
['The EU single market concerns movement of…',['Only cash','Goods, services, people and capital','Only tourists','Only farm products'],1,'These are the four freedoms associated with the single market.'],
['Which institution manages euro-area monetary policy?',['Every school separately','The CCPC','Each shop owner','The European Central Bank'],3,'The ECB is responsible for monetary policy for the euro area.'],
['A benefit of using the same currency with a trading partner is…',['No need to exchange those currencies','All products become free','All prices become identical','No business rules apply'],0,'A shared currency removes exchange between those currencies, although other costs remain.'],
['A balanced evaluation of EU membership should consider…',['Benefits only','Costs only','Market access, opportunities, obligations and competition','Only the flag design'],2,'Evaluating means weighing advantages and challenges with reasons.']]);
add('economy-case','economy','Think like an economist','⚑','3.10, 3.11','Make a judgement you can back up.',
'When evaluating a policy, identify the objective, likely benefits, costs and who is affected. Use evidence, check its source and date, and separate a fact from an opinion. A strong conclusion explains a trade-off and justifies a recommendation. Economic issues often have more than one reasonable view.',
'A town considers a €2m bus scheme. Possible benefits include better access and less congestion. Costs include funding and disruption. A balanced judgement asks who uses the service, who pays and whether the benefits justify the cost.',[
['Which is the strongest start to evaluating the bus scheme?',['Assume everyone benefits equally','Ignore funding','Identify the goal, costs and affected groups','Only repeat a slogan'],2,'Start with what the policy aims to achieve and who gains or bears costs.'],
['Which is evidence rather than an unsupported opinion?',['A dated passenger count from a named study','Everyone obviously loves buses','This is definitely the best plan ever','No one will disagree'],0,'A named, dated source allows you to check the claim and its relevance.'],
['A possible cost of the scheme is…',['More transport choices','Reduced congestion','Better access to work','Public funds unavailable for another use'],3,'Using limited public money has an opportunity cost.'],
['Which conclusion is best justified?',['Build it because I say so','Support it if measured access benefits justify costs, with a review after launch','There can never be costs','One person’s view proves everything'],1,'A justified recommendation connects evidence, trade-offs and a way to review results.']]);
